{"tool_name":"trainable_weka_segmentation","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"Trainable_Weka_Segmentation","home":"https://imagej.net/Trainable_Weka_Segmentation","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Machine learning tool for microscopy pixel classification.","addition_date":"2019-07-08T06:26:12Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T21:08:28.485978Z","tool_type":["Desktop application"],"version":["3.2.33"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:Trainable_Weka_Segmentation","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"ignacio.arganda@ehu.eus","name":"Ignacio Arganda-Carreras","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://imagej.net/Trainable_Weka_Segmentation"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"maturity":"Mature","name":"Trainable Weka Segmentation","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"Ruta","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btx180","metadata":{"abstract":"Summary: State-of-the-art light and electron microscopes are capable of acquiring large image datasets, but quantitatively evaluating the data often involves manually annotating structures of interest. This process is time-consuming and often a major bottleneck in the evaluation pipeline. To overcome this problem, we have introduced the Trainable Weka Segmentation (TWS), a machine learning tool that leverages a limited number of manual annotations in order to train a classifier and segment the remaining data automatically. In addition, TWS can provide unsupervised segmentation learning schemes (clustering) and can be customized to employ user-designed image features or classifiers.","authors":[{"name":"Arganda-Carreras I."},{"name":"Cardona A."},{"name":"Eliceiri K.W."},{"name":"Kaynig V."},{"name":"Rueden C."},{"name":"Schindelin J."},{"name":"Seung H.S."}],"citationCount":1448,"date":"2017-08-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"Trainable Weka Segmentation